        "partners_title": "A few producers we love",
        "partners_intro": "To discover these wines, a few winegrowers and wine cooperatives we particularly like:",
        "partners_rieme": "Morteau · soft drinks",
        "fruitiere_title": "Did you know?",
        "fruitiere_text": "In Franche-Comté, a <em>fruitière</em> is a cooperative. Dairy farmers pool their milk there to make Comté; winegrowers do the same with their grapes in the <em>fruitières vinicoles</em>. Same word, same idea: producing together, in the village.",
        "rieme_title": "Maison Rième, Morteau",
        "rieme_intro": "Since 1921, Maison Rième has been making lemonades and syrups in Morteau, in the heart of the Haut-Doubs. Founded by Marcel Rième and his brother-in-law Adrien Bouhéret, it has always remained in the family, now in its fourth generation.",
        "r1_tag": "Craft lemonade",
        "r1_secret": "Its name pays tribute to the <em>Mortuaciens</em>, the people of Morteau. Created by Marcel Rième, it became the emblem of the house, and its recipe is still a closely guarded secret.",
        "r1_taste": "Sparkling and thirst-quenching.",
        "r1_table": "For the whole family, after a hike, or with a Cancoillotte.",
        "r2_name": "Fir syrup",
        "r2_badge": "Our gift on arrival",
        "r2_secret": "The fir is the tree of the Haut-Doubs: it covers the forests around the apartment and scents the smoke of the tuyés. Maison Rième has turned it into a syrup, which we offer you for your stay.",
        "r2_enjoy": "Diluted with cold still or sparkling water.",
        "band_village_title": "To discover in the village",
        "band_village_sub": "Cheeses and cured meats of the Haut-Doubs",
        "village_note": "You will find them a short walk away, from the village's producers and shops (see <a href='#trouver'>Where to buy</a>).",
        "fromages_title": "Cheeses of the Haut-Doubs",
        "fromages_intro": "Here, cheese is a matter of raw milk, meadows and cooperatives. Farmers deliver their milk to the village <em>fruitière</em>, which turns it into cheese on site. Four cheeses sum up this know-how.",
        "c1_tag": "AOP · cooked pressed cheese",
        "c1_secret": "It takes about 400 litres of raw milk from Montbéliarde or Simmental cows to make a 40 kg wheel. Ripening lasts at least four months, often much longer (eight months on average, sometimes two years): every wheel has its own character.",
        "c1_taste": "Young, it is mild and milky, with notes of butter and hazelnut. With age it becomes fruitier and more powerful, and develops small crystals.",
        "c1_table": "Chardonnay with a young Comté, Savagnin with an aged one: the region's most famous pairing.",
        "c2_tag": "AOP · soft cheese, bound in spruce",
        "c2_secret": "The winter cheese. It is only made from 15 August to 15 March, and only sold from 10 September to 10 May. As soon as it is made, it is bound with a strip of spruce bark, then ripened in its spruce-wood box, which gives it its woody aroma.",
        "c2_taste": "Creamy enough to eat with a spoon, with notes of undergrowth and resin.",
        "c2_table": "Baked in the oven as a “boîte chaude”, with a dash of white wine and potatoes. And a glass of Savagnin.",
        "c3_tag": "AOP · uncooked pressed cheese",
        "c3_secret": "You can recognise it by its black line. According to tradition, farmers protected the curd from a first milking with a thin layer of soot, until they could complete the cheese with milk from the next milking. Today the line is made of tasteless vegetable charcoal, and it has remained as a signature.",
        "c3_taste": "A supple, melting texture with a mild, milky taste that grows stronger with age.",
        "c3_table": "Chardonnay or Poulsard. Melted as raclette, it works wonders.",
        "c4_tag": "IGP · melted cheese",
        "c4_secret": "The most surprising of all. The milk is skimmed to make a small cheese, the <em>metton</em>, which is left to ripen before being melted. The result is a runny cheese cream, awarded IGP status in 2022.",
        "c4_taste": "Plain, with garlic or with wine, cold or warm, on bread or hot potatoes.",
        "c4_table": "A fresh Chardonnay, or a Mortuacienne.",
        "salaisons_title": "Cured meats smoked in the tuyé",
        "tuye_title": "The tuyé",
        "tuye_text": "In the old farmhouses of the Haut-Doubs, a huge pyramid-shaped wooden chimney dominates the house: this is the tuyé. Hams and sausages are hung there above a slow fire of softwood and sawdust, which dries them and gives them their flavour. This tradition is listed in France's inventory of intangible cultural heritage.",
        "s1_name": "Morteau sausage",
        "s1_tag": "IGP · smoked over softwood",
        "s1_secret": "The queen of the region's cured meats, slowly smoked over softwood and sawdust. You can recognise it by its little wooden peg. And one detail links the whole terroir: the pigs are partly fed on whey from the local cheese dairies.",
        "s1_taste": "Firm, smoky, very aromatic. It is cooked gently in simmering water, without pricking it, so that it keeps its juices.",
        "s1_table": "Lentils, potatoes, Cancoillotte… and a Poulsard or a Trousseau.",
        "s2_name": "Montbéliard sausage",
        "s2_tag": "IGP · smoked, seasoned with cumin",
        "s2_secret": "Its cousin from the Montbéliard area: slightly curved, seasoned with cumin and pepper, and also smoked over softwood.",
        "s2_taste": "Finer than the Morteau, juicy, with the warm touch of cumin.",
        "s2_table": "In a potato salad or with hot Cancoillotte, and a rosé or a Poulsard.",
        "s3_name": "Smoked ham and brési",
        "s3_tag": "Smoked and dried",
        "s3_lbl_secret": "Their secret",
        "s3_secret": "Haut-Doubs ham goes through salt, the smoke of the tuyé, then long months of drying. Brési is a speciality of salted, dried and smoked beef, cut into very thin slices.",
        "s3_taste": "The ham is tender and deeply smoky. Brési is lean, concentrated and dark red.",
        "s3_table": "On a plate, with a Poulsard or a Trousseau. Brési as an aperitif, with a Crémant.",
        "accords_title": "Franche-Comté pairings",
        "accords_intro": "The raclette and fondue sets are in the kitchen, the cheeses and cured meats in the village (we introduce them <a href='#fromages'>further down</a>). Here, for each dish, is the Jura wine that goes well with it.",
